---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Resize Images in Your Browser - FreeFileTool"
  description="Learn how to resize images client‑side with privacy‑first processing, using the FreeFileTool Resize tool."
>
  <section class="guide-hero py-12 text-center">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Resize Images in Your Browser</h1>
    <p class="text-lg mb-6">A step‑by‑step guide on using the FreeFileTool Resize Images tool.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose">
    <h2>Why Resize Images?</h2>
    <p>Resizing images reduces file size, speeds up page loads, and saves bandwidth while keeping visual quality. It’s essential for web performance, email attachments, and social media.</p>

    <h2>When to Use Browser‑Side Resizing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Quick adjustments without installing software.</li>
      <li>Privacy‑sensitive images where you don’t want to upload to a server.</li>
      <li>Batch processing of a handful of photos.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Use the Tool</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Visit the <a href="/image/resize/">Resize Images</a> page.</li>
      <li>Drag‑and‑drop or select the image(s) you wish to resize.</li>
      <li>Enter the desired width and/or height in pixels. Leave a field blank to keep the original dimension.</li>
      <li>Optional: Check the "Lock aspect ratio" box to preserve the original proportion.</li>
      <li>Click the <strong>Download</strong> button for each image, or use the <strong>Download All</strong> ZIP option.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Tips</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>For best quality, keep the larger dimension and let the tool calculate the other.</li>
      <li>If you need further compression after resizing, consider using the <a href="/compress-image/">Image Compressor</a> tool.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
